{"id": "q0242", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 42.36, "n_chunks": 43, "scores": [0.7507, 0.6493, 0.3759, 0.6503, 0.6557, 0.6905, 0.637, 0.5259, 0.6247, 0.6652, 0.3898, 0.638, 0.6125, 0.4834, 0.6042, 0.7295, 0.7173, 0.6307, 0.5242, 0.6576, 0.6442, 0.6387, 0.63, 0.5816, 0.4853, 0.5123, 0.6653, 0.5082, 0.6204, 0.6404, 0.6493, 0.7292, 0.6464, 0.6918, 0.5283, 0.6734, 0.7392, 0.5549, 0.544, 0.8389, 0.7584, 0.6822, 0.5714], "eot_score": 0.3237, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": "B. 65,000 feet", "query": "The lowest point on Earth is the bottom of the Mariana Trench at a depth of 35,840 feet below sea level. The highest point on Earth is the summit of Mt. Everest at a height of 29,028 feet above sea level. Which of the following is the best estimate of the distance between the lowest and highest points on Earth?\n\nA. 75,000 feet\nB. 65,000 feet\nC. 55,000 feet\nD. 60,000 feet\nE. 7,000 feet\nF. 64,000 feet\nG. 70,000 feet\nH. 50,000 feet\nI. 6,000 feet\nJ. 80,000 feet\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "The lowest point on Earth is the bottom of the Mariana Trench at a depth of thirty five thousand eight hundred and forty feet below sea level. The highest point on Earth is the summit of Mount Everest at a height of twenty nine thousand twenty eight feet above sea level. Which of the following is the best estimate of the distance between the lowest and highest points on Earth? (A) seventy five thousand feet, (B) sixty five thousand feet, (C) fifty five thousand feet, (D) sixty thousand feet, (E) seven thousand feet, (F) sixty four thousand feet, (G) seventy thousand feet, (H) fifty thousand feet, (I) six thousand feet, (J) eighty thousand feet. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "first_audio_ms": 553, "spoken_s": 26.08, "mode": "local", "answer": "To estimate the distance between the lowest and highest points on Earth, add the depth of the Mariana Trench and the height of Mount Everest relative to sea level. The trench is 35,840 feet below sea level, and Everest is 29,028 feet above sea level. Adding these gives approximately 64,868 feet. The closest option is F. 64,000 feet. Answer F. 64,000 feet.", "answer_ms": 11063}
{"id": "q0248", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 13.84, "n_chunks": 14, "scores": [1.0, 0.8146, 0.7792, 0.778, 0.8244, 0.8313, 0.8396, 0.9143, 0.8965, 0.9305, 0.9011, 0.919, 0.8549, 0.8461], "eot_score": 0.8347, "eot_read_ms": 26, "reference_answer": "A. Lhf", "query": "Select the best translation into predicate logic. Holly loves Frances. (f: Frances; h: Holly; Lxy: x loves y)\n\nA. Lhf\nB. hLf\nC. hLh\nD. Lfh\nE. LfLh\nF. fLhL\nG. fLf\nH. hLfL\nI. fLh\nJ. LfhL\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Holly loves Frances. Frances H. Holly. ∀x∀y (L(x,y)). A) ∀x∀y (L(x,y)). B) ∃x∃y (L(x,y)). C) ∃x∀y (L(x,y)). D) ∀x∃y (L(x,y)).", "first_audio_ms": 25, "stall_pcm_s": 3.6, "relay_first_audio_ms": 648, "spoken_s": 4.52, "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The correct translation is A. ∀x∀y L[x,y].", "expert_answer": "A) ∀x∀y (L(x,y))", "expert_latency_s": 2.56, "stall_ms": 25, "relay_ms": 1974}
{"id": "q0256", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 58.91, "n_chunks": 59, "scores": [0.6664, 0.8246, 0.8597, 0.7664, 0.7095, 0.8294, 0.6859, 0.924, 0.8242, 0.8501, 0.827, 0.8676, 0.7282, 0.802, 0.7852, 0.7784, 0.7375, 0.7914, 0.7304, 0.7453, 0.7791, 0.6106, 0.7871, 0.7564, 0.7902, 0.5801, 0.7121, 0.5182, 0.701, 0.7682, 0.7393, 0.7227, 0.7228, 0.7708, 0.7464, 0.6682, 0.6671, 0.7448, 0.7577, 0.6654, 0.7193, 0.7719, 0.7497, 0.8154, 0.6656, 0.7164, 0.774, 0.7916, 0.7732, 0.748, 0.7565, 0.7802, 0.7833, 0.8167, 0.7366, 0.7446, 0.7866, 0.5395, 0.7976], "eot_score": 0.7235, "eot_read_ms": 25, "reference_answer": "B. i_forced= 24cos1000t - 12 sin 1000tmA", "query": "A voltage source 60cos1000t V is in series with a 2-k\\Omega resistor and a 1 -\\muFcapacitor. Findi_forced.\n\nA. i_forced= 28cos1000t - 14 sin 1000tmA\nB. i_forced= 24cos1000t - 12 sin 1000tmA\nC. i_forced= 20cos1000t + 10 sin 1000tmA\nD. i_forced= 30cos1000t - 15 sin 1000tmA\nE. i_forced= 15cos1000t - 30 sin 1000tmA\nF. i_forced= 30cos1000t + 15 sin 1000tmA\nG. i_forced= 24cos1000t + 12 sin 1000tmA\nH. i_forced= 20cos1000t - 10 sin 1000tmA\nI. i_forced= 26cos1000t - 13 sin 1000tmA\nJ. i_forced= 28cos1000t + 14 sin 1000tmA\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A voltage source 60 cos(1000t) V is in series with a 2 kΩ resistor and a 1-μF capacitor. Find iforce. (a) iforce = 28 cos(1000t) - 14 sin(1000t) mA. (b) iforce = 24 cos(1000t) + 12 sin(1000t) mA. (c) iforce = 20 cos(1000t) + 10 sin(1000t) mA. (d) iforce = 30 cos(1000t) - 15 sin(1000t) mA. (e) iforce = 15 cos(1000t) - 30 sin(1000t) mA. (f) iforce = 30 cos(1000t) + 15 sin(1000t) mA. (g) iforce = 24 cos(1000t) + 12 sin(1000t) mA. (h) iforce = 20 cos(1000t) - 10 sin(1000t) mA. (i) iforce = 26 cos(1000t) - 13 sin(1000t) mA. (j) iforce = 28 cos(1000t) + 14 sin(1000t) mA. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "first_audio_ms": 23, "stall_pcm_s": 3.6, "relay_first_audio_ms": 676, "spoken_s": 20.0, "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Actually, none of the options exactly matches the correct answer. The forced current is $i_{\\text{force}} = 24\\cos{1000t} - 12\\sin{1000t}\\ \\text{mA}$.", "expert_answer": "None of the listed options is exactly correct.\n\nCorrect current: **\\(i_{\\text{force}} = 24\\cos(1000t) - 12\\sin(1000t)\\ \\text{mA}\\)**.", "expert_latency_s": 10.84, "stall_ms": 23, "relay_ms": 9085}
{"id": "q0268", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 20.16, "n_chunks": 21, "scores": [0.9999, 0.7273, 0.7944, 0.7344, 0.8005, 0.8266, 0.7409, 0.792, 0.716, 0.7127, 0.6816, 0.6758, 0.7268, 0.7061, 0.6153, 0.6573, 0.696, 0.8056, 0.7853, 0.8581, 0.7559], "eot_score": 0.7231, "eot_read_ms": 23, "reference_answer": "F. 20%", "query": "The percentage of children in Vietnam (age 8) who reported physical punishment by teachers in the past week in 2009 was about what?\n\nA. 60%\nB. 30%\nC. 10%\nD. 40%\nE. 70%\nF. 20%\nG. 90%\nH. 50%\nI. 80%\nJ. 100%\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "The percentage of children in Vietnam aged eight who reported physical punishment by teachers in the past week in two thousand nine was about what? (A) sixty percent, (B) thirty percent, (C) ten percent, (D) forty percent, (E) seventy percent, (F) twenty percent, (G) ninety percent, (H) fifty percent, (I) eighty percent, (J) one hundred percent.", "first_audio_ms": 22, "stall_pcm_s": 3.6, "relay_first_audio_ms": 629, "spoken_s": 9.8, "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The percentage of children in Vietnam aged 8 who reported physical punishment by teachers in the past week in 2009 was about twenty percent.", "expert_answer": "(F) twenty percent", "expert_latency_s": 5.03, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 4084}
{"id": "q0273", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 23.07, "n_chunks": 24, "scores": [0.643, 0.7329, 0.6247, 0.6871, 0.6409, 0.5128, 0.717, 0.7383, 0.704, 0.5357, 0.7583, 0.6193, 0.6429, 0.5945, 0.6597, 0.5826, 0.7591, 0.8432, 0.8033, 0.7999, 0.7624, 0.7998, 0.7276, 0.7054], "eot_score": 0.4566, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "D. x = 3 and x = 2", "query": "Use the quadratic formula to solve for x in the equation x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0.\n\nA. x = -1 and x = 6\nB. x = 0 and x = 6\nC. x = 1 and x = 2\nD. x = 3 and x = 2\nE. x = 1 and x = 6\nF. x = 3 and x = 4\nG. x = 2.5 and x = -2.4\nH. x = -2 and x = -3\nI. x = 4 and x = 5\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Use the quadratic formula to solve for x in the equation x squared minus five x plus six equals zero. a) x equals minus one and x equals six, b) x equals zero and x equals six, c) x equals one and x equals three and x equals two, d) x equals one and x equals six, f) x equals three and x equals four, g) x equals two pi and x equals minus two pi, h) x equals minus two and x equals three, i) x equals four and x equals five. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "first_audio_ms": 546, "spoken_s": 146.72, "mode": "local", "answer": "We are given the quadratic equation $x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0$. To solve using the quadratic formula, recall that for an equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, the solutions are $x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$. Here, $a = 1$, $b = -5$, and $c = 6$. Plugging in these values, we get $x = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 - 24}}{2}$, which simplifies to $x = \\frac{5 \\pm 1}{2}$. So, $x = 3 \\text{ or } x = 2$. The correct option is C. Answer:C$x=1 \\text{ and } x=3 \\text{ and } x=2$.", "answer_ms": 58110}
{"id": "q0288", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 115.11, "n_chunks": 116, "scores": [0.9972, 0.9901, 0.5838, 0.6545, 0.7551, 0.7197, 0.6864, 0.764, 0.657, 0.7577, 0.7537, 0.5652, 0.7093, 0.8035, 0.728, 0.8528, 0.7934, 0.8345, 0.734, 0.7842, 0.6763, 0.8166, 0.7536, 0.7145, 0.6751, 0.7066, 0.6211, 0.7247, 0.7602, 0.7073, 0.6815, 0.6853, 0.8067, 0.7222, 0.7139, 0.7236, 0.6914, 0.6808, 0.6893, 0.7183, 0.5346, 0.6846, 0.7902, 0.5825, 0.6571, 0.7627, 0.6816, 0.7517, 0.7359, 0.6928, 0.7299, 0.6546, 0.6298, 0.6782, 0.6185, 0.7241, 0.6665, 0.6929, 0.7345, 0.7696, 0.7229, 0.7036, 0.6612, 0.6704, 0.732, 0.4309, 0.683, 0.771, 0.6838, 0.7749, 0.5232, 0.6975, 0.6255, 0.6781, 0.7585, 0.7714, 0.7504, 0.7238, 0.6527, 0.5014, 0.7908, 0.7061, 0.7823, 0.6291, 0.6414, 0.7335, 0.7131, 0.7735, 0.7407, 0.7385, 0.7042, 0.7472, 0.7468, 0.7738, 0.7008, 0.7797, 0.7174, 0.7498, 0.6941, 0.6835, 0.5967, 0.5787, 0.6304, 0.749, 0.8027, 0.7406, 0.816, 0.8173, 0.7567, 0.6716, 0.688, 0.7591, 0.7015, 0.7601, 0.6173, 0.6881], "eot_score": 0.4565, "eot_read_ms": 23, "reference_answer": "G. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car.", "query": "A highway patrol officer stopped a driver for speeding and found that the person had a suspended license. He allowed her husband to drive the car home but arrested her for driving under suspension. After securing her inside the squad car, he conducted a search of her vehicle. The officer found a bag of cocaine inside a zippered jacket on the back seat. He arrested her for possession and possession with intent to deliver a controlled substance. Prior to trial, the driver moved for suppression of the cocaine. Is it likely that the trial court will grant the suppression motion?\n\nA. Yes, the motion will be granted because a search of a vehicle requires a search warrant unless there are exceptional exigent circumstances.\nB. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have the driver's consent to search the car.\nC. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had the right to search the car for officer safety reasons.\nD. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had a reasonable suspicion to search the car due to the driver's suspended license.\nE. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had probable cause to search the car after finding out about the suspended license.\nF. No, the motion will be denied because this was a valid search incident to an arrest.\nG. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car.\nH. Yes, the motion will be granted because the cocaine was found in a personal item, not in plain sight.\nI. No, the motion will be denied because there is no expectation of privacy toward property inside one's car.\nJ. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have a warrant to search the jacket on the back seat.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A highway patrol officer stopped a driver for speeding and found that the person had a suspended license. He allowed her husband to drive the car home but arrested her for driving under suspension. After securing her inside the squad car, he conducted a search of her vehicle. The officer found a bag of cocaine inside a zippered jacket on the back seat. He arrested her for possession and possession with intent to deliver a controlled substance. Prior to trial, the driver moved for suppression of the cocaine. Is it likely that the trial court will grant the suppression motion? A. Yes, the motion will be granted because a search of a vehicle requires a search warrant unless there are exceptional exigent circumstances. B. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have the driver's consent to search the car. C. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had the right to search the car for officer safety reasons. D. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had a reasonable suspicion to search the car due to the driver's suspended license. E. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had probable cause to search the car after finding out about the suspended license. F. No, the motion will be denied because this was a valid search incident to an arrest. G. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car. H. Yes, the motion will be granted because the cocaine was found in a personal item not in plain sight. I. No, the motion will be denied because there is no expectation of privacy toward property inside one's car. J. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have a warrant to search the jacket on the back seat.", "first_audio_ms": 593, "spoken_s": 5.32, "mode": "local", "answer": "F. No, the motion will be denied because this was a valid search incident to an arrest.", "answer_ms": 2450}
{"id": "q0307", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 7.11, "n_chunks": 8, "scores": [0.7455, 0.7818, 0.7901, 0.8606, 0.8001, 0.8228, 0.7187, 0.7495], "eot_score": 0.5606, "eot_read_ms": 23, "reference_answer": "Long names", "query": "The 1968 children's story Tikki Tikki Tembo by Arlene Mosel is a humorous take on the perils of what?", "transcript": "The 1968 children's story, Tiki Tiki Tembo, by Arlene Mosel, is a humorous take on the perils of what?", "first_audio_ms": 589, "spoken_s": 39.48, "mode": "local", "answer": "The 1968 children's story *Ticky-Ticky-Temblo!* by Arlene Mosel is a humorous take on the perils of **overly rigid and excessive order and routine**—particularly in family life. The story follows young Timmy, who becomes obsessed with making everything perfectly organized and predictable to avoid chaos or stress. However, his quest for control leads to absurd situations as he enforces extreme rules at home, school, and even beyond, highlighting how an overzealous pursuit of order can ironically create more problems than it solves. The humor comes from exaggerating the consequences of being too rule-bound, making light of the anxieties that come with trying to manage life's unpredictability.", "answer_ms": 17787}
